= Preface
Throughout the last decades, Walter Rudin's _Principles of Mathematical Analysis_ has become the standard when it comes to Analysis.
I see too many Analysis books seeking to prove with dire speed propositions while others avoid true rigor under the "expense of being pedagogical".

More than over ninety years ago, Gödel made us aware of the incompletness of mathematics and showed that, even though all well-redacted statements of math are true or false, not all of them can be proved.

The tendency still remains ---however--- to value proof over meaning or interpretation. 
The emergence of _Artificial Intelligence_ (AI) and AI-based-proofs have disencouraged mathematicians and made some believe that mathematics has lost its meaning.

We are mourning over the death of a corpse which we have been carrying for decades: the seek for proof over all.
The value of a mathematician in modern academia was to prove, propose, and defend; 
answers were always more valuable than the questions.
I say otherwise: the most valuable is the question.

